# Kalantar et al. (2025), JGR Solid Earth, 10.1029/2025JB031938.
# Specimen OG-SH -- SHEAR fracture in Odenwald granodiorite.
#
# Units: m.
# Geometry (paper Table 1): length = 0.12000, diameter = 0.04998,
# fracture angle = 29 degrees, measured from the specimen's long/vertical axis to
# the fracture plane.
#
# WHY 29 DEGREES IS TRUSTED HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not because Table 1 prints it. The paper's own equations (3), (4) and (5),
#
#     sigma'_n = (sigma_3 - P_p) + (sigma_1 - sigma_3) sin^2(theta)
#     tau      =                   (sigma_1 - sigma_3) sin(theta)cos(theta)
#     P_p      = (P_i + P_o)/2,     P_o = 3 MPa throughout
#
# give the identity   tan(theta) = (sigma'_n - sigma_3 + P_p) / tau,  in which every
# term is tabulated. Regressing Table 2's nine OG-SH hold stages on that identity
# returns theta = 29.006 deg and sigma_3 = 32.996 MPa with a maximum residual of
# 0.004 MPa. Two constants reproduce the entire stress table, so this is a
# recovery, not a fit. See scripts/kalantar_parameter_audit.py section 1.
#
# The same audit shows sigma_3 = 33.00 MPa for all three specimens, which is the
# effective confining pressure of 30 MPa quoted in section 2.3 plus the 3 MPa pore
# pressure at the start of injection. That is the number the deck's confining
# boundary condition must carry -- NOT 30 MPa.
#
# This matters because the Ye & Ghassemi (2018) campaign found one specimen
# (SW-T2) whose printed angle its own data table contradicts, and that error was
# built into a mesh and run for months before anyone divided (3) by (4). OG-SH is
# clean. OG-T is not -- see OGT/mesh/, where the recovery returns 26.0 against a
# printed 28.0.
#
# Plane, centred at z = L/2 = 0.060, at x = +/- r = 0.02499:
#     z = L/2 +/- r cot(29 deg) = 0.060 +/- 0.045083153
#     z_high = 0.105083153      z_low = 0.014916847
# The elliptical fracture spans 90.17 mm of the 120.00 mm core, clearing each end
# face by 14.92 mm.
#
# Expected fracture_interface area after the split: pi r^2 / sin(29 deg)
#     = 4.046795e-3 m^2.
#
# AFTER REGENERATING, pin the injection/production coordinates onto real interface
# nodes. Section 2.3 places two 2 mm boreholes 5 mm from the sidewall, one at each
# end, so the intended source points are at x = +/- (r - 0.005) = +/- 0.019990:
#     source_in   coord = '-0.019990 0 0.023937085'
#     source_out  coord = ' 0.019990 0 0.096062915'
# These are where the boreholes SHOULD be. ExtraNodesetGenerator with
# use_closest_node = true will silently snap them to the nearest node, which may be
# several mm away or -- worse -- a bulk node off the fracture entirely. Run
# scripts/check_source_nodes.py against the exported mesh and write the exact node
# coordinates back into the deck before any production run.
#
# ============================================================================
# CHECKED 2026-08-23 -- THE FACTOR-4 MESH FAILS THIS TEST. USE FACTOR 3.
# ============================================================================
# This is the exact failure mode the warning above describes, and it is the only
# one of the four Kalantar meshes to hit it.
#
#   kalantar2025_og_sh_theta29_size4.e   30,600 elements,  937 interface nodes,
#                                        interface pitch 1.504 mm
#       Both sources snap to a BULK node 950.7 / 933.5 um away. The nearest node
#       that is actually on the fracture is 1217.0 um away -- FURTHER than the
#       bulk node -- so use_closest_node loses the fracture and the injection
#       drives the matrix. The run would complete and be wrong.
#
#   kalantar2025_og_sh_theta29_size3.e  100,048 elements, 1977 interface nodes,
#                                        interface pitch 1.035 mm
#       Both sources land ON the fracture, 4.1 um from the design point. This is
#       the production mesh for OG-SH.
#
# VERIFIED SOURCE COORDINATES for the factor-3 mesh. USE THESE IN THE DECK:
#     source_in   coord = '-0.019992000 0 0.023933478'
#     source_out  coord = ' 0.019992000 0 0.096066522'
# Borehole 4.998 mm from the sidewall against a 5.000 mm design; separation
# 82.4736 mm, +0.010 %, a +0.002 % bias on Q. Effectively exact.
#
# IF THE FACTOR-4 MESH IS USED ANYWAY (it is 3.3x cheaper), the sources MUST be
# moved onto the interface by hand:
#     source_in   coord = '-0.020580000 0 0.022872698'
#     source_out  coord = ' 0.020580000 0 0.097127302'
# That puts the borehole 4.410 mm from the sidewall and the separation at 84.8993
# mm, +2.951 %, a +0.669 % bias on Q. The bias is small and correctable. The reason
# to prefer factor 3 anyway is not the bias: OG-SH carries the smallest slip signal
# in the paper -- 42.0 um total, largest single step 11.0 um -- distributed across
# five holds, and it is the specimen the campaign's rate-independence weakness will
# be tested on. Resolving the smallest observable on the coarsest fracture is the
# wrong trade even when the flow error is negligible.
#
# Geometry of both meshes is exact: L = 120.00 mm, D = 49.98 mm, theta = 29.000 deg,
# plane-fit residual 0.00 um, fracture_interface area 4.046794e-3 m^2 against the
# 4.046795e-3 derived above.
#
# NOTE ON THE 5 mm INSET: the paper says the holes are "5 mm from the sidewall" for
# a 2 mm diameter hole, which is ambiguous between the hole centre and its near
# edge. This journal takes it as the centre. If it is the edge, x becomes
# +/- 0.018990 and the source z values shift by 1.804 mm; the flow path length
# changes by 5 %, and Q enters the comparison through the pressure drop, so this is
# a first-order ambiguity worth resolving from the data release before calibrating.
#
# MESH SIZE. "auto factor" is a Cubit coarseness index, not a length, and it is
# applied to the whole volume at once. Factor 5 is the production discretisation used
# by every reported Ye2018 run; factor 3 is the convergence sibling, about an order
# of magnitude more elements. Set the factor, then export under the matching name.
#
# An attempt to size the fracture and the bulk INDEPENDENTLY was made on 2026-08-24,
# measured, and rejected. The result is recorded above the sizing command below --
# read it before trying the same thing.

reset
create Cylinder height 0.12000 radius 0.02499
move Volume 1  z 0.060 include_merged

create vertex location 0.02499 0.02499 0.105083153
create vertex location 0.02499 -0.02499 0.105083153
create vertex location -0.02499 -0.02499 0.014916847
webcut volume 1 with plane vertex 3 vertex 4 vertex 5
delete vertex 3 4 5
webcut volume all with plane xplane imprint merge
webcut volume all with plane yplane imprint merge

# ==============================================================================
# BOREHOLE VERTEX IMPRINT -- a mesh node at each source BY CONSTRUCTION.
# Added 2026-08-24. NOT YET RUN IN CUBIT -- see "IF THE SPLIT FAILS" below.
# ==============================================================================
# WHY. Both boreholes sit at y = 0 on the fracture plane. That is not the interior
# of a surface: it is exactly the line where the yplane webcut above cut the
# fracture ellipse -- the ellipse's MAJOR AXIS -- and it is a geometric curve.
# Cubit divides that curve into N EQUAL intervals (measured: min spacing == max
# spacing to machine precision), so the only node positions the source can reach
# are k/N along it. The design borehole sits at x/r = 0.01999/0.02499 = 0.799920,
# so the pinning error is integer arithmetic, round(0.79992 N)/N - 0.79992:
#
#     mesh              N    nearest fraction     pinning error
#     _size3  OG-SH     25   20/25 = 0.800000         4.1 um   <-- N divisible by 5
#     _size3  OG-T      27   22/27 = 0.814815       792.9 um
#     _size3  OG-SC     26   21/26 = 0.807692       388.5 um
#     graded  OG-SH     28   22/28 = 0.785714       732.2 um
#     graded  OG-T      29   23/29 = 0.793103       362.8 um
#     graded  OG-SC     27   22/27 = 0.814815       744.4 um
#
# All six reproduce the independently measured check_source_nodes.py distance to
# 0.1 um. So THIS MESH'S CELEBRATED 4.1 um PIN WAS NOT QUALITY -- it is 25 being
# divisible by 5, because the design borehole sits two microns off exactly 4/5 of
# the radius. Nothing else about this mesh is special, and the two failures in the
# graded attempt were the same arithmetic, not a meshing defect.
#
# Splitting the curve at the design position puts a VERTEX there, and Cubit meshes
# every vertex with a node. Pinning then becomes EXACT and INDEPENDENT of mesh
# size, which is the thing that lets this mesh be coarsened at all.
#
# The split adds one vertex and replaces one curve with two. It creates no new
# surfaces and no new volumes, so the hardcoded block/nodeset surface IDs and the
# nodeset 5/6 vertex IDs further down are unaffected. Do NOT try to do this with a
# webcut instead -- that renumbers everything.
#
# CURVE IDS: run Examples/Kalantar2025/mesh_probe_axis_curves.jou once and paste
# them here. They are the two curves of length 51.5460 mm (= r/sin29) lying in
# surfaces 26 46 53 32; that length is unique in the model. c_pos runs to +x
# (towards z_high = 0.105083153), c_neg to -x (towards z_low = 0.014916847).
# Left at 0 deliberately, so an unfilled journal fails loudly on "curve 0".
#{ c_pos = 0 }
#{ c_neg = 0 }
split curve {c_pos} location position  0.019990 0 0.096062915
split curve {c_neg} location position -0.019990 0 0.023937085
#
# IF THE SPLIT FAILS -- if Cubit rejects `split curve` on merged geometry, or the
# mesh quality drops near the new vertex -- comment the two split lines out and
# uncomment the two below instead. Forcing N divisible by 5 puts a node at exactly
# 0.8 r = 0.019992, i.e. 4.1 um from design, at ANY global coarseness, with no
# change to the topology at all. That is the same pin this mesh already has, made
# deliberate instead of accidental.
#   curve {c_pos} {c_neg} interval 25
#   curve {c_pos} {c_neg} scheme equal
# ==============================================================================

imprint all
merge all

# ==============================================================================
# GRADED SIZING: TRIED 2026-08-24, MEASURED, AND REJECTED. Do not try it again
# without reading this. The premise was wrong, not the execution.
# ==============================================================================
# The idea was to size the fracture and the bulk independently -- fracture 1.00 mm,
# bulk 1.80 mm -- on the theory that this mesh was over-refined in the bulk and
# ~45,000 elements could be recovered from rock where nothing happens.
#
# IT WAS NOT. Median node spacing [mm] against distance from the fracture plane,
# measured on the exported meshes:
#
#     distance      0-1   1-2   2-4   4-8  8-16  16-32  32-100 mm
#     _size3.e     0.968 0.988 0.986 1.004 0.977 0.955  0.978
#     graded       0.918 0.906 0.931 0.926 0.872 0.846  0.877
#
# Both profiles are FLAT. The mesh was already uniform at ~0.98 mm everywhere out to
# 100 mm from the fracture; there was no over-refined bulk to recover. And
# `volume all size 0.00180` had no effect at all -- the 1.00 mm SURFACE size
# propagated through the entire volume and made the whole mesh finer.
#
# The reason is the element type. `scheme polyhedron` produces HEX8 here, and a hex
# mesher propagates surface intervals along the mapped directions through the whole
# volume. It cannot grade away from an interior surface the way a tet mesher can.
# Refining the fracture necessarily refines everything.
#
# WHAT IT COST, measured:
#     elements       100,048 -> 108,240   (+8.2 %, the WRONG way)
#     interface nodes  1,977 ->   2,521   (+27.5 %)
#     interface pitch  1.035 ->   0.942 mm
#     source pinning   PASSES  ->  FAILS  -- both boreholes snap to BULK nodes
#                                            707.6 um away, with the nearest
#                                            interface node 732.2 um away
#     re-pinned separation 82.4736 -> 81.0009 mm, so mesh_flow_width_over_length
#     moves +1.82 % on a SCORED channel, to buy an 8 % SLOWER mesh.
#
# HOW THE WRONG PREMISE WAS REACHED, so it is not repeated: the "implied bulk edge"
# was inferred as (volume/n_elements)^(1/3) = 1.33 mm and compared against the
# interface pitch of 1.035 mm, and the 1.29 ratio was read as weak grading. Those
# two numbers are not commensurable -- one is a 3D element-volume scale, the other a
# 2D in-plane node spacing -- so the ratio was an artefact of the comparison, not a
# property of the mesh. The spacing-versus-distance profile above is the direct
# measurement, it costs one command, and it should have been run first.
#
# IF THIS MESH EVER NEEDS TO BE CHEAPER: it cannot be done by grading with this
# scheme, only by coarsening globally -- and factor 4 already fails source pinning
# outright (see the block at the top). The lever is not the mesh. Use 128 ranks.
# The durable fix would be to imprint the two borehole vertices into the geometry so
# a node exists at each source BY CONSTRUCTION, which would make source pinning
# exact and mesh-independent and allow free coarsening. That is a geometry change
# and has not been attempted.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACTIVE SIZING: the production mesh, uniform, factor 3.
vol all size auto factor 3   # 4 -> _size4.e (FAILS source pinning) ; 3 -> _size3.e
volume all scheme polyhedron
mesh volume all

# The rejected graded pair, kept only so the measurement above can be reproduced.
# If you uncomment these, change the export name at the bottom to _graded.e:
#   volume all size 0.00180
#   surface 26 46 53 32 size 0.00100

list totals   # element count

block 1 add volume 2 4 8 6
block 1 name "top_block"

block 2 add volume 1 3 5 7
block 2 name "bottom_block"

# The surface and vertex IDs below are the ones Cubit assigns to this exact
# construction order, and are shared verbatim by all four Ye2018 journals. They are
# the one thing in this file that cannot be checked without running Cubit: if the
# version in use numbers differently, the nodesets will be silently wrong rather
# than error. Confirm "fracture_interface" has the area quoted in the header before
# exporting.
nodeset 1 add surface 36 56 63 42
nodeset 1 name "top_nodeset"
nodeset 2 add surface 28 30 50 48
nodeset 2 name "bottom_nodeset"
nodeset 3 add surface 33 43 61 51 25 35 57 47
nodeset 3 name "sides_nodeset"
nodeset 4 add surface 26 46 53 32
nodeset 4 name "fracture_interface"
nodeset 5 add vertex 27 34 41 48
nodeset 5 name "no_disp_x"
nodeset 6 add vertex 10 11 18 19
nodeset 6 name "no_disp_y"

# VERIFY BEFORE EXPORTING. Element and interface-node counts will now differ from
# the pre-imprint mesh (100,048 / 1977) because the split adds a constrained vertex;
# the fracture AREA must not move -- it is the same geometry.
list nodeset 4                          # interface nodes
list surface 26 46 53 32 geometry       # fracture area, expect 4.046795e-3 m^2

# EXPORTED UNDER A NEW NAME ON PURPOSE. _size3.e is the mesh the 110-series decks
# point at and jobs 110_02 / 110_06 are running on it right now; do not overwrite
# it with an untested mesh. Promote _pin.e to production only after BOTH of these
# pass, run from the repository root in the `moose` conda environment:
#     python3 scripts/check_axis_intervals.py <this mesh>   -> expect 0.0 um
#     python3 scripts/check_source_nodes.py  <this mesh> \
#             -0.019990 0 0.023937085   0.019990 0 0.096062915
# The second must report the sources ON the fracture interface. If it does, the
# deck's source coordinates become the DESIGN values above -- no re-pinning, and
# mesh_flow_width_over_length goes to the design separation of 82.4654 mm.
export mesh "kalantar2025_og_sh_theta29_size3_pin.e" overwrite
